  "evidence": "Authority/Loyalty: TEXT_F — “final‑say referendum…implement the people’s decision,” rebuild neighbourhood policing, maintain NATO and renew Trident, assertive public ownership of key utilities; TEXT_D — Stop Brexit/revoke Article 50, lead with allies via EU/UN/NATO, curb mass surveillance and police facial recognition, decriminalise cannabis/abortion, PR and Lords reform. Equality: TEXT_F — £10 Real Living Wage for all, sectoral bargaining, abolish tuition fees and restore maintenance, scrap Universal Credit and punitive sanctions, over 1m social homes with rent controls; TEXT_D — fix Universal Credit, 300k homes/year incl. 100k social, 1p on income tax for NHS/social care, broaden equality protections. Proportionality: TEXT_D — Skills Wallets for lifelong learning, new dependent‑contractor status, prompt‑payment enforcement, land‑value‑based levy and action on multinational avoidance; TEXT_F — raise income tax only above £80k but also 32‑hour week with no loss of pay and Inclusive Ownership Funds. Care: TEXT_F — National Care Service with free personal care, expand mental health/public health, Right to Food, end rough sleeping; TEXT_D — mental‑health parity with waiting standards and 24/7 crisis care, support for carers, end rough sleeping, public‑health focus.",
  "reasoning": "Because Authority and Loyalty are top‑weighted in the profile, TEXT_F more closely honors procedural authority and national solidarity (final‑say referendum with implementation, stronger policing, NATO/Trident) whereas TEXT_D’s revoke‑first Brexit stance and civil‑liberties curbs on policing read as more subversive of traditional authority. On secondary priorities, both satisfy Care, but TEXT_F leans more heavily into Equality, while TEXT_D better fits Proportionality; given the higher weights on Authority/Loyalty and Equality, TEXT_F aligns more closely overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
